    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p><strong>Table&nbsp;9.01</strong> Targeted immunopanels and potential pitfalls when determining cancer site of origin</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Ashley Cimino-Mathews",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 616.65pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"822\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Primary tumour</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Pertinent positive immunohistochemistry </span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Pertinent negative immunohistochemistry </span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Tumours with potential aberrant/overlapping immunohistochemistry patterns</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>ER+ breast cancer</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{35461734;36980510;36649695;39243111;31335489;18469795}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"SV\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: SV;\">ER, CK7 (87%), TRPS1 (95%), GATA3 (80%), mammaglobin (77%), GCDFP-15 (70%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">PAX8 (59&ndash;95%<sup>a</sup>), WT1 (90%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"FR\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: FR;\">Gynaecological tumours (ER+, PAX8+, WT1+)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Skin adnexal and salivary gland tumours (ER+/&minus;, GATA3+)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">ER&minus; breast cancer </span></strong><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{23260325;21411781;30628926;30468800;35413381;27058032;33011748;21107096}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">TRPS1 (90%), GATA3 (30&ndash;66%), SOX10 (40&ndash;80%), CK</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">ER, TTF1 (95%), napsin&nbsp;A (87%), uroplakin, CD45 (LCA)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"PT\" style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-ansi-language: PT;\">Melanoma (SOX10)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"PT\" style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-ansi-language: PT;\">Urothelial carcinoma (GATA3+, uroplakin+)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (morphological overlap; CD45+, cytokeratin&minus;)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Melanoma</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{8121183;18949364}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">S100, SOX10, HMB45, melan&nbsp;A, MITF</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cytokeratin (98%), TRPS1 (93.5%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Triple-negative breast cancer can be S100+/SOX10+</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Melanoma can be CK+ (2&ndash;10%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Lung adenocarcinoma </span></strong><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{11845849;18300807;30628926;36702357;21107096}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">CK7, TTF1, napsin&nbsp;A</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"SV\" style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-ansi-language: SV;\">ER+ (&lt;&nbsp;50%), GCDFP-15 (95%), mammaglobin (60%), GATA3 (90%), SOX10 (95%), TRPS1 (75%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Breast cancer may rarely be TTF1+ (&lt;&nbsp;5%) and napsin&nbsp;A+ (13%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>High-grade serous carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{39105782;31335489;35417734;18469795}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CK7, ER (80%), WT1 (90%), PAX8 (95%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"SV\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: SV;\">GCDFP-15 (95%), mammaglobin (82%), GATA3 (90%), SOX10 (95%), TRPS1 (46&ndash;79%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Breast cancer can be WT1+ (10% of mucinous and micropapillary cancers) and PAX8+<sup>a</sup></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 6; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Skin adnexal neoplasms</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{38545085;1951842}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"SV\" style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-ansi-language: SV;\">CK7, GCDFP-15 (90%), mammaglobin (5%), GATA3 (71%), SOX10 (50%), TRPS1 (86%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">ER (73%, in eccrine-apocrine carcinomas)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">D2-40+/&minus;, CK14+/&minus;, and CK15+/&minus;</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 7; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Colorectal adenocarcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CK20, CDX2</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"SV\" style=\"mso-ansi-language: SV;\">ER, GCDFP-15, mammaglobin, GATA3, SOX10, TRPS1</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">May be CK7+/&minus; (&lt;&nbsp;10%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 8; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Poorly cohesive gastric carcinoma</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">CK7, CK20, and CDX2 variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">ER, GCDFP-15, mammaglobin, GATA3, SOX10, TRPS1, E-cadherin</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span lang=\"PT\" style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-ansi-language: PT;\">Invasive lobular carcinoma (CK7+, E-cadherin&ndash;negative/aberrant)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 9; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Prostate cancer</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-ID\">{20863373;24996432;37965687}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">PSA, P501S, NKX3-1, AR</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">ER, GATA3 (97.5%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Invasive lobular carcinoma and male invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST) can be NXK3-1+ (27.3%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 10; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.35pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"290\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Neuroendocrine tumours (NETs) and neuroendocrine carcinomas (NECs) of non-breast origin</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variably synaptophysin+, chromogranin&nbsp;A+, INSM1+; CD56, other non-breast markers (CDX2, PAX8, TTF1)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">ER</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"177\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Breast NETs and NECs are ER+</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup>a</sup>Clone dependent: &lt;&nbsp;5% with BC12 versus 41% with MRQ-50 antibody clone.</p>",
